Problem & Constraints: 50 GB total storage. Logs must not exceed 10 GB. System stability and service availability must be maintained.
Partition Layout (LVM Recommended):
./ (Root): 15 GB (Operating system binaries, config files)
./var/log: 10 GB (Dedicated volume ensures log growth cannot freeze / or /home)
./home or /data: 20 GB (User files and application data)
.swap: 5 GB (System memory management)
Log Rotation Strategy (/etc/logrotate.d/app-logs):
command:
/var/log/app/*.log {
    daily
    rotate 7
    maxsize 10G
    size 1G
    compress
    missingok
    notifempty
    postrotate
        /usr/bin/systemctl reload app-service > /dev/null 2>&1 || true
    endscript
}
Justification: Isolating /var/log into its own LVM partition guarantees that even if logs fill up to 10 GB, critical system processes writing to / or application data written to /home will continue operating without disk exhaustion failure.
