Scheduler Tuning:
The Linux scheduler decides which process or thread gets CPU time. Scheduler parameters can be tuned to reduce switching overhead.
Adjust scheduling policy: Use policies such as SCHED_FIFO or SCHED_RR for real-time tasks.
Set process priority: nice and renice can give important processes higher CPU priority.
CPU affinity: Bind critical processes to specific CPU cores using taskset, reducing unnecessary migration between CPUs.
Reduce unnecessary preemption: Adjust kernel preemption settings when workloads contain CPU-intensive tasks.
Real-time scheduling: Real-time processes can be given higher priority so that time-sensitive operations execute with minimal delay.
Kernel Modules:
Kernel modules can improve performance by extending kernel functionality without rebuilding the entire kernel.
Unnecessary modules can be disabled or unloaded to reduce kernel overhead.
Custom modules can provide optimized hardware or I/O handling.
Modules can be designed to handle specific workloads more efficiently.
However, poorly designed modules may cause crashes or increase kernel overhead, so they should be carefully tested.
