This runs the program on CPU core 2, reducing CPU migration and potentially reducing context-switching overhead.
3Q)
Proposed Approach:
Assume the log file is server.log and we need to extract useful information such as error counts, IP addresses, status codes, or timestamps.
An efficient shell script can use awk, grep, sort, and uniq rather than processing each line using a shell loop.
#!/bin/bash
LOGFILE="server.log"
echo "Total Entries:"
wc -l < "$LOGFILE"
echo "Error Entries:"
awk '$0 ~ /ERROR/' "$LOGFILE" | wc -l
echo "HTTP Status Code Summary:"
awk '{count[$9]++} END {
    for (code in count)
        print code, count[code]
}' "$LOGFILE"
echo "Top 10 IP Addresses:"
awk '{count[$1]++} END {
    for (ip in count)
        print count[ip], ip
}' "$LOGFILE" | sort -nr | head -10
4Q)
When a faulty process consumes 90% of CPU, the administrator should first identify the process, monitor its behavior, and then control or terminate it without rebooting the system. Critical services must be protected throughout the process.
command:
ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head -6
read -p "Enter PID: " PID
ps -p $PID -o pid,user,%cpu,%mem,stat,cmd
top -b -n 5 -p $PID
pidstat -p $PID 1 5
renice +10 -p $PID
ps -p $PID -o pid,user,%cpu,%mem,ni,stat,cmd
kill $PID
sleep 3
ps -p $PID
kill -9 $PID
top -b -n 1 | head -15
The administrator should not immediately reboot the system. The preferred strategy is to identify the faulty PID using top/ps, verify that it is not a critical process, and then reduce its priority or impose a CPU limit. If the process remains problematic, it should be gracefully terminated using kill, with kill -9 used only when necessary. Continuous monitoring ensures that critical services remain available while CPU utilization returns to normal.
