To ensure smooth operation:
Dynamic Memory Allocation: Allow VMs to receive additional memory when required.
Memory Ballooning: Reclaim unused memory from less-active VMs and provide it to VMs under higher load.
Memory Overcommitment: Can be used carefully if workloads are not simultaneously memory-intensive.
Memory Reservation: Reserve at least 2 GB for each VM so that the minimum requirement is always satisfied.
Host Reservation: Keep 4 GB dedicated to the host OS to prevent the host from becoming unstable.
Conclusion
The server can successfully host all 5 VMs without exceeding the available 16 GB RAM. Allocating 4 GB to the host and 2.4 GB to each VM is a simple and safe strategy. For workloads with varying memory demands, dynamic allocation and memory ballooning can further improve resource utilization while maintaining the minimum 2 GB requirement for each VM.
